                                    <h1>How Logistics Companies Get More Enquiries Online: The 90-Day Launch Plan (2026) | ITD GrowthLabs</h1>
                                    <div style="background:#fff7ed;border-left:4px solid #ff5722;padding:20px 24px;border-radius:10px;margin:24px 0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.7;color:#334155;"><strong style="color:#ea580c;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:0.4px;">ITD LOGISTICS PORTFOLIO &mdash; THE EVIDENCE:</strong><br>This article is written from live category work &mdash; not desk research. ITD GrowthLabs works with <a href="/case-studies/courier-management-system.php" style="color:#ea580c;font-weight:700;">CourierDost</a> as a live logistics client, has delivered 5 logistics SaaS engagements (<a href="/case-studies/courier-management-system.php" style="color:#ea580c;font-weight:700;">Courier Management System</a>, <a href="/case-studies/cross-border-courier-platform-saas.php" style="color:#ea580c;font-weight:700;">Cross-Border Courier Platform</a>, <a href="/case-studies/fleet-management-saas-3pl-logistics.php" style="color:#ea580c;font-weight:700;">3PL Fleet Management</a>, <a href="/case-studies/on-demand-courier-booking-app.php" style="color:#ea580c;font-weight:700;">On-Demand Courier Booking</a>, <a href="/case-studies/smart-logistics-saas-platform.php" style="color:#ea580c;font-weight:700;">Smart Logistics SaaS</a>), and ships productised platforms including <a href="/products/courier-management-software.php" style="color:#ea580c;font-weight:700;">Courier Management Software</a> and <a href="/products/logistics-mobile-app.php" style="color:#ea580c;font-weight:700;">Logistics Mobile App</a>. Our founder built Trackmate Lite, a logistics SaaS in production use. That means every framework below is grounded in operator context &mdash; AWB flow, POD, hub-and-spoke routing, cross-border compliance &mdash; not generic B2B marketing theory.</div><p>Most logistics operators want the same thing when they hire an agency &mdash; more enquiries. Not "awareness", not "brand lift", not "reach". Enquiries. Real people asking real quote or capacity questions with real money to spend.</p><p>This article is the 90-day enquiry-launch plan we run for logistics engagements: what happens each week, what goes live in what order, and where the first meaningful enquiries come from. It is the exact sequence we recommend regardless of whether you are a courier operator, 3PL, freight forwarder or cold-chain specialist. Adapt the channel weights (see our <a href="/resources/digital_marketing_strategy_for_logistics_companies.php">strategy playbook</a>) for your specific buyer archetype.</p>
                                    <h2>Weeks 1-2: Discovery + funnel diagnostic</h2>
                                    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Fully understand your existing traffic, existing leads, existing conversion path, and identify the 3-5 highest-leverage fixes. No campaigns launched yet.</p><p><strong>Activities:</strong></p><ul style="padding-left:20px;line-height:1.9;"><li>Google Search Console + Analytics audit &mdash; where does existing traffic come from and where does it drop off?</li><li>Website heat-mapping (Hotjar or Microsoft Clarity) on 5-10 highest-traffic pages.</li><li>CRM audit &mdash; what does the existing pipeline look like + where do closed deals originate?</li><li>Competitor audit &mdash; top 5 direct competitors + top 5 category-adjacent players. Backlinks + rankings + paid-ad activity.</li><li>Positioning workshop with the founder / marketing head &mdash; who are we for, who are we NOT for, what is the differentiator.</li><li>Buyer-archetype prioritisation &mdash; which 1-2 archetypes drive Phase 1 (see strategy playbook).</li></ul><p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> 30-page growth diagnostic + 90-day plan + first 3 quick wins slated.</p>
                                    <h2>Weeks 3-4: Foundation &amp;mdash; website + tracking + WhatsApp + CRM</h2>
                                    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Every channel we launch after week 4 needs to convert. Foundation weeks are about closing the biggest conversion leaks.</p><p><strong>Activities:</strong></p><ul style="padding-left:20px;line-height:1.9;"><li>Website conversion fixes (top 3-5 from the 9-point audit &mdash; usually form redesign, mobile CTAs, schema deployment, tracking widget performance, hero + trust-signal rework).</li><li>Google Business Profile creation / optimisation for every operating hub.</li><li>WhatsApp Cloud API + Interakt (or AiSensy) setup with lead-capture flow + tracking-notification flow live.</li><li>CRM wiring (HubSpot or Zoho) &mdash; every form / WhatsApp / paid-ad lead lands in CRM within seconds with source attribution.</li><li>Google Ads + Meta Ads Manager + LinkedIn Campaign Manager account setup + pixel + conversion tracking.</li><li>1-2 dedicated buyer-archetype landing pages (D2C-3PL page, or enterprise-3PL page, or freight-forwarder-route page &mdash; whichever aligns with the priority archetype).</li></ul><p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> Production-ready growth foundation.</p>
                                    <h2>Weeks 5-6: Paid launch &amp;mdash; Google Search + WhatsApp</h2>
                                    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> First paid channel live. Learning phase begins. First few enquiries land within 2 weeks.</p><p><strong>Activities:</strong></p><ul style="padding-left:20px;line-height:1.9;"><li>Google Search Ads: 3-5 high-intent commercial keywords per archetype, tightly matched. Rs 25-50K spend for weeks 5-6 to establish baseline CPC + CPA.</li><li>WhatsApp click-to-message ads on Meta: 1-2 lead-magnet creatives (rate card, quote form, tour booking) with tight audience.</li><li>Landing pages A/B tested from day 1 (headline + form structure + trust strip).</li><li>Sales team briefed on qualification questions + response SLA (target: 30 minutes for D2C, 4 hours for enterprise, tracked in CRM).</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> First 10-30 enquiries by end of week 6. Quality varies; conversion follow-ups start immediately.</p>
                                    <h2>Weeks 7-8: SEO + content launch</h2>
                                    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Editorial engine starts publishing. First non-brand organic impressions in month 3.</p><p><strong>Activities:</strong></p><ul style="padding-left:20px;line-height:1.9;"><li>4 initial blog articles published (2 buyer-selection guides, 1 cost + rate breakdown, 1 city + hub deep-dive).</li><li>Service silo restructure live (individual service pages, individual buyer pages, individual city + hub pages).</li><li>Schema deployment sitewide (Organization + LocalBusiness + Service + FAQPage + BreadcrumbList).</li><li>2-3 backlink outreach campaigns started (industry publication guest posts, directory citations, partner cross-linking).</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> Traffic still small from organic (SEO compounds). Google Search Console impressions +30-60% by end of week 8.</p>
                                    <h2>Weeks 9-10: LinkedIn ABM launch (if enterprise-priority)</h2>
                                    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Enterprise ABM sequences activated. First qualified InMail replies.</p><p><strong>Activities:</strong></p><ul style="padding-left:20px;line-height:1.9;"><li>Target account list finalised (50-150 accounts for mid-market, 150-500 for enterprise).</li><li>LinkedIn Sales Nav sequences set up (3-6 touch cadence over 6-12 weeks).</li><li>Gated content assets published (whitepaper + case study + rate + capacity brief).</li><li>LinkedIn Ads campaigns launched targeting the TAL with content-first creative.</li><li>Founder / leadership LinkedIn posting cadence starts (2-3 posts / week).</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome:</strong> First 15-30 connection acceptances + 2-5 qualified InMail replies by end of week 10.</p>
                                    <h2>Weeks 11-12: Optimise + amplify</h2>
                                    <p><strong>Goal:</strong> First round of optimisation across all channels. Paid channels start hitting target ROAS. Content amplification loop live.</p><p><strong>Activities:</strong></p><ul style="padding-left:20px;line-height:1.9;"><li>Paid channels: cut lowest-performing keywords + audiences + creative. Scale spend on winners by 25-50%.</li><li>Content amplification: each published article gets a 2-3 week amplification tail (LinkedIn distribution + WhatsApp broadcast + partner cross-promotion).</li><li>Referral programme launch &mdash; existing customer referrals with tracked links.</li><li>First case study published + amplified (based on a Week 6-8 conversion).</li><li>90-day review call with founder / CMO &mdash; metrics, milestones, next-quarter plan.</li></ul><p><strong>Expected outcome by end of day 90:</strong> 30-80 qualified enquiries generated over the quarter, first case study live, first non-brand organic traffic showing up in Search Console, paid channels at or approaching target CPL, WhatsApp CRM integrated, LinkedIn ABM cadence humming.</p>
                                    <h2>What NOT to do in the first 90 days</h2>
                                    <p>Consistent traps we see logistics operators fall into during the first 90 days of a marketing programme:</p><ul style="padding-left:20px;line-height:1.9;"><li><strong>Launching 5 channels at once.</strong> None get enough attention or spend to optimise. Sequence matters.</li><li><strong>Skipping the CRM foundation.</strong> Without proper CRM wiring, you cannot measure ROAS or improve the funnel. Lead volume is worthless without close-rate data.</li><li><strong>Content before positioning.</strong> Publishing generic "logistics trends" content before nailing buyer segmentation wastes cycles and confuses SEO signals.</li><li><strong>Chasing vanity metrics.</strong> Impressions + CTR + likes are noise. Track SQLs + closed-won revenue attribution.</li><li><strong>Cutting the programme at month 3.</strong> Month 3 is when things look worst &mdash; foundation costs paid, SEO not yet compounding, paid still optimising. Hold discipline through month 6 to see the compound layer emerge.</li></ul>

                                    <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a1a2e 0%, #16213e 100%); padding: 40px; border-radius: 12px; margin: 40px 0; text-align: center; color: #fff;">
                                        <h3 style="color:#fff;margin-bottom:15px;">Ready to Get Started?</h3>
                                        <p style="color:#ccc;margin-bottom:25px;">Book a free 30-minute logistics-marketing discovery call. We map your funnel + share a customised proposal within 3 working days.</p>
                                        <a href="https://www.itdgrowthlabs.com/contact-us.php" style="background:#ff5722;color:#fff;padding:14px 32px;border-radius:6px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;display:inline-block;margin-right:10px;">Contact Us Today</a>
                                        <a href="https://calendly.com/itdgrowthlabs-info/30min" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="js-book-call" data-source="blog_cta" style="background:#1565c0;color:#fff;padding:14px 32px;border-radius:6px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:600;display:inline-block;">Book Free 30-min Call</a>
                                    </div>

                                    <div class="faq-section" style="margin-top:40px;">
                                        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                                        <h3>How fast can a logistics company start getting enquiries from digital marketing?</h3>
                                        <p>First enquiries typically arrive in week 5-6 from paid channels (Google Search + WhatsApp click-to-message ads). SEO + content generates first organic enquiries in month 3-4. LinkedIn ABM generates first qualified InMail replies in week 10-12. Cumulative 30-80 qualified enquiries by end of day 90 is typical for a well-executed 90-day launch.</p>
                                        <h3>Do we need to fix the website before running ads?</h3>
                                        <p>Yes, at minimum the top 3-5 conversion leaks from the 9-point audit (form redesign, mobile CTAs, schema, tracking widget performance, hero + trust strip). Running paid on a leaky website wastes 30-60% of ad spend. A 2-week foundation phase is worth it even under time pressure.</p>
                                        <h3>Should we launch Google Ads or Meta Ads first?</h3>
                                        <p>Google Search Ads for most logistics operators because intent is higher and conversion is faster. Meta Ads (including WhatsApp click-to-message) works in parallel for D2C-facing courier + 3PL operators. Sequence: launch Google Search week 5, add Meta week 6, add LinkedIn week 9 for enterprise archetypes.</p>
                                        <h3>What is a realistic first-90-day KPI target?</h3>
                                        <p>30-80 qualified enquiries cumulative across all channels; first non-brand organic impressions live in Search Console; first case study published; paid channels approaching target CPL; CRM fully wired with source attribution. Closed-won revenue is early &mdash; typically month 4-6 for D2C, month 6-9 for enterprise.</p>
                                        <h3>How much of the 90-day budget goes to paid vs content vs website?</h3>
                                        <p>Rough shape: 20% foundation (website + CRM + WhatsApp + tracking), 15% content + SEO (first 6-8 articles + schema + on-page), 55-60% paid (Google + Meta + LinkedIn ad spend), 5-10% tools + tooling licence. Adjust weights by buyer archetype &mdash; enterprise-focused programmes tilt toward LinkedIn + content; D2C-focused programmes tilt toward Meta + WhatsApp.</p>
                                        <h3>What happens after day 90?</h3>
                                        <p>Days 91-180: scale winners + retire losers on paid channels; SEO + content compounds; case studies stack; LinkedIn ABM enters second-touch cadence; retention loops (WhatsApp broadcast + email nurture) become measurable revenue drivers. Day 90 is when the programme becomes self-improving; day 180 is when it delivers material contribution margin.</p>
                                        <h3>Can we run this 90-day launch in-house without an agency?</h3>
                                        <p>In principle yes; in practice, few logistics companies have the specialist skills (paid buying + SEO + WhatsApp Cloud API + LinkedIn ABM + content editorial) in one team. Most that try in-house get 2-3 channels running well and the rest stall. Agency partnership makes sense for the first 12-18 months to build the muscle; hybrid model (in-house senior + agency execution) works after that.</p>
                                        <h3>What tools do we need in place before starting the 90-day plan?</h3>
                                        <p>GA4 + Google Tag Manager + Meta Pixel + LinkedIn Insight Tag + WhatsApp Cloud API + Interakt or AiSensy (Rs 5-25K / month) + a CRM (HubSpot Starter Rs 4-15K / month or Zoho CRM equivalent) + LinkedIn Sales Nav (Rs 6-10K / user / month). Total tooling: Rs 20K-Rs 65K / month depending on team size + tier.</p>
